# Security Rules

## Authentication Flow

### JWT Strategy
- **Access token**: short-lived (15 min), sent as `Authorization: Bearer <token>` header
- **Refresh token**: long-lived (30 days), stored in `httpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Strict` cookie only
- **Never** store access token in localStorage — keep it in memory (JS variable in the Svelte store)
- On page refresh: call `POST /api/auth/refresh` with the cookie to get a new access token

### JWT Payload
```rust
#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct Claims {
    pub sub: String,     // user_id
    pub email: String,
    pub exp: usize,      // expiry timestamp
    pub iat: usize,      // issued at
}
```

### Refresh Token
- Store only a bcrypt hash of the refresh token in DB (not the token itself)
- On use: verify hash, issue new access token, rotate refresh token (issue new one, invalidate old)
- On logout: delete refresh token from DB

## Authorization Checks
Every handler that accesses a resource must verify:

1. **User is authenticated** (JWT middleware handles this)
2. **User is a member of the workspace** that owns the resource
3. **User has the right role** for the action:
   - `viewer`: read only — cannot create/edit/delete anything
   - `member`: read + create + edit own items + delete own items
   - `admin`: full access including invite/remove members, delete board

```rust
// Pattern for every handler:
async fn update_card(
    State(state): State<AppState>,
    Extension(auth_user): Extension<AuthUser>,
    Path(card_id): Path<String>,
    Json(body): Json<UpdateCardRequest>,
) -> Result<impl IntoResponse, AppError> {
    // 1. Fetch card to get board_id
    // 2. Fetch board to get workspace_id
    // 3. Check auth_user is member of that workspace with sufficient role
    // 4. Then proceed
}
```

## Input Validation Rules
- Validate all request bodies before any DB operation
- Max lengths:
  - Names (workspace, board, list): 255 chars
  - Card title: 500 chars
  - Card description: 50,000 chars
  - Comment body: 10,000 chars
  - Label name: 100 chars
- Reject empty strings where a value is required
- Sanitize filenames for attachments (strip path separators, null bytes)
- Validate UUIDs format before using in queries

## File Upload Security
```rust
const ALLOWED_MIME_TYPES: &[&str] = &[
    "image/jpeg", "image/png", "image/gif", "image/webp",
    "application/pdf",
    "text/plain", "text/csv",
    "application/zip",
    "application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.sheet",
    "application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document",
];
```
- Verify MIME type from file content (magic bytes), not just the Content-Type header
- Strip EXIF data from images before storing (use `image` crate)
- Store files outside the web root — never serve from a path the user can guess
- Attachment download URL: `GET /api/attachments/:id/download` (checks auth, then streams file)
- Never redirect to a file:// or local path

## HTTP Security Headers
Add via tower-http middleware:
```
X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
X-Frame-Options: DENY
X-XSS-Protection: 1; mode=block
Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'
```

## CORS
In development: allow `localhost:5173`
In production: allow only the configured `APP_URL` domain
Never use wildcard `*` in production CORS.

## Rate Limiting
Implement simple in-memory rate limiting for auth endpoints:
- `POST /api/auth/login`: max 10 attempts per IP per 15 minutes
- `POST /api/auth/register`: max 5 attempts per IP per hour
- Use a `DashMap<IpAddr, (u32, Instant)>` in AppState

## What Never to Do
- Never log passwords, tokens, or full request bodies
- Never return stack traces or internal error details to the client
- Never trust the `X-Forwarded-For` header without configuring a trusted proxy
- Never use MD5 or SHA1 for password hashing — only bcrypt (cost ≥ 12)
- Never store secrets in code — always from environment variables
- Never use `unwrap()` in handler code — always use `?` with proper error mapping
